The fix implements a Go HTTP server with a `/healthz` health-check endpoint and comprehensive E2E tests. The server exposes two endpoints:

**`/healthz`** — returns HTTP 200 with JSON health payload:
```json
{"status":"healthy","service":"foreman-tick82-e2e","version":"1.0.0"}
```

**`/`** — returns HTTP 200 with server info:
```json
{"message":"foreman-tick82-e2e server running"}
```

**`main.go`** — HTTP server with `PORT` env support (default `8080`):
```go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	"os"
)

type HealthResponse struct {
	Status  string `json:"status"`
	Service string `json:"service"`
	Version string `json:"version"`
}

func healthHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)
	json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(HealthResponse{
		Status:  "healthy",
		Service: "foreman-tick82-e2e",
		Version: "1.0.0",
	})
}

func main() {
	port := os.Getenv("PORT")
	if port == "" {
		port = "8080"
	}
	http.HandleFunc("/healthz", healthHandler)
	http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)
		json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(map[string]string{"message": "foreman-tick82-e2e server running"})
	})
	log.Printf("Starting server on :%s", port)
	if err := http.ListenAndServe(":"+port, nil); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("Server failed: %v", err)
	}
}
```

**`main_test.go`** — 3 test functions with 5 test cases:
- `TestHealthEndpoint` — GET, POST, HEAD at `/healthz`; verifies status code, Content-Type, JSON body fields
- `TestRootEndpoint` — GET `/` verifies response structure
- `TestServerStartupAndHealth` — full E2E via `httptest.NewServer`, then HTTP GET `/healthz`

